ixed reality  headset called totem and then one year  later they bought akonia holographics  which was focused on making lenses for  ar glasses but these acquisitions aren't  the only way to track apple's progress  in augmented reality they also have a  ton of publicly available patent filings  and these actually date back even  further in 2008 apple patented four  different prototypes for head-mounted  displays this was years before oculus  even started and it's crazy to think  about it but facebook didn't even have a  mobile app back then these patents look  pretty basic by today's standards but  they show that apple was exploring every  possibility there was even one that  showed an iphone slotted in directly in  front of the user's eyes much like  google cardboard and gear vr would do  years later and more recently apple  patented a finger-mounted device that  will allow users to control virtual  content using natural hand movements so  apple is clearly spending a ton of time  and effort on research and development  here but people often overlook what  apple is doing right in front of our  noses see apple might be delaying the  launch of a real pair of ar glasses but  they aren't holding back on adding those  innovations to their current products  every year the iphone the ipad and the  apple watch become slightly more  powerful and it can be easy as a  consumer to miss what's really going on  apple has been slowly shrinking every  piece of technology and including more  sensors in each iteration for example  lidar sensors are extremely valuable  when you're trying to map an environment  in augmented reality and apple has been  able to get this technology in the hands  of hundreds of millions of people it  started with the top tier ipad but now  lidar ships with each new iphone pro  these phone-enabled experiences are  still far cry from the immersive worlds  that zuckerberg has promised but apple  has an army of independent app  developers who are eager to capitalize  on every new piece of technology apple  deploys this wouldn't be such a problem  for zuck if apple products weren't so  popular see globally only about 20  percent of people carry iphones but  among young adults in america apple's  market share is 70  so when developers want to release the  next big app they look to apple  immediately instagram is a perfect  example here they wanted to capture that  young american market so they went a  full two years without an android app at  all this is exactly what apple wants to  have happen and if this strategy works  the metaverse will just slowly develop  on top of apple products and they will  essentially win by default but this  strategy is a double-edged sword that  could wind up killing the metaverse  before it even gets started and to  understand why we have to look at this  one simple button it's the app store  purchase button and if you've ever used  an iphone you're probably very familiar  with it but behind this simple design  lies one of the most controversial  business models in history it wasn't  controversial at launch though when  steve jobs first introduced the app  store to the public in 2008 the audience  cheered there is no charge for free apps  at all there's no charge to the user it  seemed like an amazing deal after all  there was no way for developers to load  custom software onto iphones at the time  and the app store had no hosting fees no  marketing fees and no credit card  processing fees but the next decade  would change everything smartphones  became ubiquitous and every business  needed to launch an iphone app and fast  the app store worked perfectly for  companies with advertising based  business models in 2018 facebook and  google controlled eight of the top 10  smartphone apps in the us and because  these apps were free to download apple  didn't get a cut of the revenue they  generated from advertising but things  are changing increasingly people are  willing to pay for services that they  love and they don't want to be  constantly advertised to just look at  fortnite the game is wildly successful  and often pointed to as an excellent  bridge to the metaverse what started  exclusively as a competitive shooter is  now regularly hosting concerts in a few  years i wouldn't be surprised if you can  do a whole lot more there's only one  thing standing in the way though apple's  app store fees even though games like  fortnite are free to download apple  still takes a 30 cut of in-app purchases  this might have seemed reasonable in  2008 when the iphone was still brand new  but when developers have the option of  going direct and paying just three  percent to a service like stripe 30  seems exorbitant and here's the crazy  thing apple actually admitted that they  couldn't possibly expect to hold on to  their precious 30 cut forever in 2011 an  apple executive wrote an email  suggesting that apple should consider  lowering the fee once they started  making over 1 billion dollars per year  well that never happened and apple made  85 billion dollars from the app store  last year way beyond that proposed cap  so why does this matter and how does it  affect zuckerberg well we have to  clarify our definition of the metaverse  a bit here see i don't think it's fair  to just throw on a vr headset and say  that you're in the metaverse that's cool  and all but it's not quite what people  mean by the term it's best to throw out  the buzzwords and just ask the question  what will the internet look like in 10  years will vr be a part of it almost  certainly but there are going to be a  ton of other important developments the  type of content that can be streamed  will change thanks to faster wireless  connectivity more real-world data will  be represented virtually enabling the  type of ar experiences that apple is  working on and entire economies will  develop online this last part is really  critical we're already seeing some of  this happen with crypto communities and  game development is increasingly funded  by the sale of virtual goods as soon as  people can start trading digital items  and moving them from one virtual world  to another markets for these items  should flourish but apple wants to take  a 30 cut of every transaction that  happens on an ios device and that could  wind up crushing innovation when apple  launched the app store they promoted it  with an amazing ad campaign called  there's an app for that it was  remarkably catchy and introduced the  world to the idea of apps as friendly  and helpful tools that they could easily  download one big benefit of the app  store was that every piece of software  was reviewed by apple now will there be  limitations of course there are going to  be some apps that we're not going to  distribute porn malicious apps apps that  invade your privacy so there will be  some apps that we're going to say no to  that safety guarantee from apple was a  huge boon to user adoption everyone from  children to grandparents felt  comfortable downloading and using apps  but apple didn't just restrict malicious  apps they also put up barriers to  competition steve jobs made this crystal  clear in an email to his team when he  wrote i think this is all pretty simple  ibooks is going to be the only bookstore  on ios devices adoption of the metaverse  will most likely be driven by today's  young adults and 70 of them are  currently using iphones if people end up  wanting to use their iphones and apple  watches to interact with the metaverse  zuckerberg is going to have to fork over  30 of his profits apple is pressuring  zuckerberg on two fronts right now  they're leveraging their top-tier  product development teams to build  competitive ar hardware and then  standing strong on their app store fees  to ensure they get a cut of mobile  revenue going forward but there's  actually one more reason why apple is in  such a strong position and to understand  it we have to go back nearly 40 years in  